8,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"GB","distance":0.6903,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"SEA","distance":0.7138,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jaguars went 9-8 in 2022 and outscored opponents 404 to 350. The offense ranked 8th in EPA per play and the defense 13th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 2nd highest screen rate and the 3rd most linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Jaguars offense was well above average by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 2nd highest screen rate in the league. Trevor Lawrence ran the passing game, with Christian Kirk leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Jaguars defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 6th and 21st. Josh Hines-Allen was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Dawuane Smoot, who had 5 sacks despite playing only 44% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd most linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Jaguars were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 86% of field goals, which was about average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 43% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Trevor Lawrence played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,113 yards. Christian Kirk was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 1,108 receiving yards. Travis Etienne handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, Josh Hines-Allen led the team with 7 sacks and Tyson Campbell picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Raiders under Chip Kelly and the 2024 Bears. Their defense looked most like the 2019 Commanders and the 2025 Titans under Dennard Wilson.","trends":"The offense went from 26th in EPA per play in 2021 to 8th in 2022, a big step forward. The defense moved from 31st in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 13th in 2022, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was two-high safety rate, which went from 59% to 95%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}